<div class="page-intro">
  <p class="kicker">A standing project · github.com/diewan</p>
  <h1>Diewan</h1>
  <p>Infrastructure for an accountability economy.</p>
</div>

<section>
  <p class="axiom" style="max-width:34ch;margin-bottom:2rem">
    <span class="a1">Authority stated.</span>
    <span class="a2">Action proven.</span>
    <span class="a3">Responsibility preserved.</span>
  </p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Every day we act through systems that speak for us — a clerk, a contract, a chain, an
      agent. When something goes wrong the answer arrives without a face: <em>the system does
      not allow it.</em> No mandate to inspect, no proof of what was done, no one left
      answerable. Diewan is an attempt to make that sentence impossible to say honestly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The wager is simple: a valuable digital action should carry, wherever it travels,
      independent proof of four things. Not stored in one trusted vault, but portable —
      verifiable by anyone, locally, without asking permission of the party being checked.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="ledger" aria-label="Mandate, Action, Receipt, Responsibility">
    <div class="cell">
      <p class="step">01 — Mandate</p>
      <p class="what">Authority</p>
      <p class="gloss">What was permitted, by whom, within which limits.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cell">
      <p class="step">02 — Action</p>
      <p class="what">Event</p>
      <p class="gloss">What actually occurred, materialized as evidence.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cell">
      <p class="step">03 — Receipt</p>
      <p class="what">Proof</p>
      <p class="gloss">A binding of action to mandate that anyone can check.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cell">
      <p class="step">04 — Responsibility</p>
      <p class="what">Answerability</p>
      <p class="gloss">Who remains accountable once the action is done.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

